\section{The Call as a Location Boundary}

An allocator models a call as reading argument locations, clobbering
the locations the callee is free to change, and defining result
locations. A value live \emph{through} the call must not depend on
a clobbered location for survival. It can be assigned to a preserved
register, moved to a stack slot, or recomputed from surviving inputs
if that is legal. A value passed as an argument and dead on return
has no such requirement. Keep the argument read and the call
clobber distinct: the call may both read and write the same register.

For a concrete instance, the System V \emph{AMD64 Architecture
Processor Supplement} specifies \texttt{rdi} as the first integer
argument register for a simple integer signature, \texttt{rax} for a
scalar integer result, and \texttt{rbx} as preserved. For
$y\gets f(x)$ followed by a computation of $x+y$, putting $x$ into
\texttt{rdi} satisfies the call but does not preserve $x$. Copy it
from a preserved home into \texttt{rdi}, or save it elsewhere before
the call. If the enclosing function puts $x$ in \texttt{rbx}, it now
owes preservation of \emph{its caller's} incoming \texttt{rbx}.
Chapter~\ref{ch:calling-conventions} describes the broader interface;
a register allocator consumes the relevant part as an operand and
clobber contract.

\section{Paying for Preservation on the Paths That Use It}

Chow's \emph{Minimizing Register Usage Penalty at Procedure Calls}
examines register usage information and shrink-wrapping of
callee-saved registers. Suppose a procedure is entered 100 times,
and 20 entries take a slow path that makes four calls each with
the same value live across them. A model charging one store and
one reload at each of those calls counts 160 accesses. Saving a
preserved register at entry and restoring it at return counts 200;
saving and restoring only on the slow path counts 40. This is an
illustration of placement, not a universal preference. If the
caller-saved value can stay spilled across several calls without
being read, one save and one reload can cover the group, changing
the comparison. Also count argument moves and competing demand
for the preserved register.

The invariant is that along every path which overwrites a preserved
register, the caller's original value is available for restoration
before control leaves. The saved-state description used by an
unwinder must agree with the machine state at the relevant program
points. A cold early return may remain outside the saved region;
a shared return reachable both from the region and from outside it
cannot blindly restore an uninitialized stack slot. Restore on
edges leaving the saved region or separate the return sequences.
Exercise the cold return and each supported exception or unwind path,
using distinct sentinel values in all incoming preserved registers.

\Cref{fig:visual-235} shows one way to keep the save and restore
off the path that never writes the preserved register.

