\section{Register Contracts across Modules}

Wall's \emph{Global Register Allocation at Link Time} describes
object modules carrying information about procedure and variable
use. Link-time allocation can choose registers for frequently used
variables and consider the call graph when sharing registers among
locals. The reasoning is about overlapping activations, not
whether functions were compiled from the same file. Two leaf
procedures that never have simultaneous activations can exploit
the same specialized register without destroying one another's
locals. If one can call the other while its value is live, using
that register in both requires preservation or distinct homes.
Recursive reentry can create that overlap for a procedure with
itself.

A modern summary might state which volatile registers a known target
can overwrite, including clobbers of its callees. For an indirect
call with a proved finite target set, take the union of clobbers;
an unresolved call uses the public ABI mask. Link-time summaries
must be invalidated when interposition, callbacks, or another
module version changes the possible targets. A private calling
convention can trim transfers only when all relevant callers and
callees agree; exported entries still need an ABI-compatible
interface. Compare Chapter~\ref{ch:cross-program} and the dossier's
\emph{Adaptive ABI Negotiation}: neither profiling a likely callee
nor relabeling an object-private register constitutes a proof of
the preservation contract.

